        "title": "Astronomy - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Astronomy is the science of deep time measured through light. Every observation is a measurement of *when* — when a photon left its source, how long a star has been burning, how many orbits a planet has completed, how far the expansion has stretched the wavelength. The universe has no global clock. We reconstruct time from clocks that run at different rates, in different frames, across scales that span 44 orders of magnitude (Planck time to Hubble time). This is the Timekeeper's domain."
    }
